    <a href="/" class="hover:text-blue-600">Home</a>
    <span>/</span>
    <a href="/guides" class="hover:text-blue-600">Guides</a>
    <span>/</span>
    <span class="text-gray-800 font-medium">Outdoor Shower Winterization</span>
  </nav>

  <!-- Article Header -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 mb-8 shadow-xs not-prose">
    <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-blue-50 text-blue-700 text-xs font-semibold mb-3 border border-blue-100">
      <span>❄️ Winterization Physics &amp; Freeze Protection</span>
      <span>•</span>
      <span>IPC Section 305 Freeze Defense</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 tracking-tight mb-3">
      How to Winterize an Outdoor Shower: Stop-and-Waste Valves
    </h1>
    <p class="text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
      When liquid water freezes into solid ice, its molecular lattice expands by <strong>9.05% in volume</strong>. In an enclosed copper pipe or forged brass shower valve, that volumetric expansion generates hydraulic pressures exceeding <strong>40,000 PSI</strong>—shattering metal valves with ease. Simply closing an indoor shutoff valve is not enough. Here is the step-by-step winterization protocol.
    </p>

    <!-- Quick Stats Grid -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-6 border-t border-gray-100">
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Water Ice Expansion</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900 mt-0.5">+9% Volumetric Increase</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Internal Burst Pressure</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-red-600 mt-0.5">Exceeds 40,000 PSI</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Air Blowout Pressure</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-emerald-600 mt-0.5">20 to 30 PSI Max (Safe)</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Main Editorial Content -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 sm:p-8 mb-8 prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">The 4-Step Autumn Winterization Sequence</h2>
    <p>
      Execute this 10-minute maintenance sequence before the first overnight frost (typically mid-October in northern zones):
    </p>
    <ol class="space-y-4 not-prose my-6">
      <li class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">Step 1: Close the Main Interior Shutoffs</strong>
        Locate the dedicated hot and cold isolation ball valves located inside the heated basement or utility closet and turn both handles perpendicular (90 degrees off).
      </li>
      <li class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">Step 2: Open All Outdoor Fixture Valves (Break the Vacuum)</strong>
        Walk outside and turn the outdoor shower handles to full ON (both hot and cold). If your shower has a handheld wand, lower it to ground level so gravity pulls water downward. Open any low foot-wash spigots. This breaks the atmospheric vacuum holding water in the riser pipe.
      </li>
      <li class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">Step 3: Open the Interior Stop-and-Waste Drain Caps</strong>
        Hold a 5-gallon bucket underneath your interior stop-and-waste valves. Unscrew the small knurled brass bleeder caps on the downstream side of the valves. As air enters the outdoor showerhead, water rushes back down the pipe and drains out the bleeder hole into your bucket.
      </li>
      <li class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">Step 4: Compressed Air Blowout &amp; Leave Valves Open</strong>
        Connect a small portable air compressor set to <strong>25 PSI</strong> (never exceed 40 PSI) to the bleeder valve or outdoor showerhead with a rubber air nozzle, and blow air through the line until only dry air hisses out. Leave all outdoor valves in the <strong>HALF-OPEN position</strong> all winter long.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <!-- Jobsite Field Note -->
  <FieldNote
    author="Marcus Vance"
    role="Master Systems & MEP Building Specialist"
    experience="22+ Years Field Experience"
    avatarBg="bg-amber-100 text-amber-800"
    initials="MV"
    title="Why Leaving Outdoor Valves 'Half-Open' Saves Ceramic Cartridges"
    note="Even after blowing out the pipes with compressed air, microscopic water droplets can cling to the internal rubber O-rings and ceramic discs inside your shower cartridge. If you turn the shower handle fully CLOSED for the winter, that microscopic moisture remains sealed inside the dead space between the ceramic plates. When winter temperatures drop to -10°F, that trapped pocket expands and cracks the ceramic plate! When you turn the water on in spring, the handle spins freely and water leaks continuously out of the faceplate. Always leave your outdoor shower handles turned to the '45-degree half-open' position throughout the entire winter. Leaving the valve half-open leaves all internal channels vented to the atmosphere, giving any residual moisture room to expand freely into air without exerting burst pressure on the cartridge."
  />

  <!-- Costly Mistakes -->
  <CostlyMistakes
    tradeName="Outdoor Shower Winterization"
    mistakes={[
      {
        number: 1,
        title: "Installing Standard Ball Valves Without Bleeder Drains",
        costImpact: "$850 (Water Trapped in Wall Riser Freezing)",
        description: "Standard ball valves shut off the water, but trap a column of water in the pipe leading through the exterior wall.",
        solution: "Use true stop-and-waste ball valves featuring a built-in side drain port that drains the exterior line completely dry."
      },
      {
        number: 2,
        title: "Blowing Out Lines with High-Pressure Air (> 50 PSI)",
        costImpact: "$450 (Blown-Out Ceramic Shower Cartridges)",
        description: "Using 100+ PSI from a shop air compressor blows delicate silicone check valves and ceramic cartridge seals right out of their housings.",
        solution: "Regulate your compressor down to 20 to 25 PSI; high volume at low pressure clears water safely without damaging seals."
      },
      {
        number: 3,
        title: "Leaving Handheld Shower Wands Hanging High on the Wall",
        costImpact: "$180 (Cracked Flexible Hose & Wand Split)",
        description: "Hanging a handheld wand high on its bracket traps water in the bottom loop of the flexible hose and inside the plastic spray wand.",
        solution: "Unscrew the handheld wand and hose in autumn and store it indoors in the garage or basement."
      }
    ]}
  />

  <!-- Builder Community Reviews -->
  <CommunityReviews
    tradeTitle="Shower Winterization Guide"
    reviews={[
      {
        author: "Brad & Karen S.",
        location: "Kennebunkport, ME",
        projectScope: "Coastal Outdoor Shower Freeze Protection",
        savingsClaim: "Zero Freeze Damage in -15°F Maine Winter",
        feedback: "Followed the 4-step protocol: indoor stop-and-waste opened, 25 PSI air blowout, and left the handles at 45 degrees. We endured a polar vortex with -18°F wind chills. In May, turned the water back on and the shower worked flawlessly with zero leaks.",
        keyTakeaway: "Label your indoor shutoff valves clearly with bright tags ('OUTDOOR SHOWER HOT' / 'COLD') so anyone in the family can find them in an emergency."
      },
      {
        author: "Tom H.",
        location: "Traverse City, MI",
        projectScope: "Lake Michigan Cottage Outdoor Shower",
        savingsClaim: "Saved $350 on Plumber Service Call",
        feedback: "The tip on bringing the handheld wand indoors saved my wand from cracking. The whole winterization took me exactly 8 minutes on a Saturday afternoon with a bucket and a small compressor.",
        keyTakeaway: "Keep a small plastic bag tied over the exposed outdoor showerhead to keep mud dauber wasps from building nests inside the nozzle holes."
      }
    ]}
  />

